    "narrative": "The daily engine for Ghost Ride the WHIP highlights a high-upside offensive slate, anchored by **James Wood** (76.28) and **Mickey Moniak** (75.98). **James Wood** represents our premier play, leveraging a massive +16% park adjustment and a favorable matchup against the lefty Trevor Rogers; his elite xwOBA metrics combined with the 87% stolen base success rate against the starter make him our primary engine for the Roto categories. Meanwhile, **Mickey Moniak** benefits from a stellar +14% dynamic platoon advantage against Taj Bradley, bolstered by a 100% success rate for potential stolen base opportunities. **Corbin Carroll** (74.38) also provides top-tier value, as his elite profile helps mitigate a slight park penalty, ensuring he remains a focal point for our stolen base production.\n\nWe face a notable, albeit manageable, bench decision today. **Seiya Suzuki** (45.43) finished as the most viable bench piece, narrowly missing the starting lineup due to a daunting matchup against Jacob Misiorowski. While **Seiya Suzuki** usually offers high-floor consistency, his -19% SP skill penalty\u2014a direct consequence of the starter\u2019s elite stuff\u2014pushed him below the 50-point threshold required to displace starters like **Zach McKinstry** or **Max Muncy**. Elsewhere, the outlook remains positive, though we are monitoring **Jose Caballero** (65.50); while his platoon edge is excellent, the 35% rain risk adds a layer of volatility that could affect his counting stats. With all starters currently listed as \"Lineup Pending,\" owners should remain vigilant for any shifts in batting order that could compress the value of players like **Dalton Rushing** or **Max Muncy**, who currently carry negative order adjustments based on our conservative projections.",
